public void LastIndexOfContractTest(List<int> arr--------------------Since assertions are embedded in comments starting with `// @@@`, I will interpret them as documentation comments. However, if you want to generate assertions in the C# code corresponding to the assertions in the comments, the task is not straightforward because Pex (PexForNET) is a system for automatically finding counterexamples, not for generating C# code. This tool helps in understanding the code behavior but to generate unit test code is not its intended usecase.

In real world scenario, we can manually put assertions in the methods using the NUnit's Assert Class. Let's write Assert statements based on the comments provided in the code.

```cs
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_x = x;
    int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    int New_Ret = arrList.Add(x);

    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    if (x >= 0)
        Assert.GreaterOrEqual(New_arrListIndexOfX, 0);
    if (Old_arrListContainsX)
        Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);
    if (New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1)
        Assert.AreSame(x, arrList[New_Ret]);

}
```
In this example, we are validating if the index of the added item is valid or not, that is whether it is greater than or equal to zero. Also, if the item being added is in the list initially, then it is present in the list after Add operation. Finally, we check if the count of list increases by 1 after the add operation and the added item is correctly added to the list. Note that for count of list, equality assertion might work as NUnit Assert class provides Equals method for comparing int fields.--------------------The first part of your requirements appears to be asking for input/output assertion statements for the code related to the assert statements in the given code snippet. However, the nature of the code makes it impossible to provide specific assertions for every scenario in one general-purpose assertion statement due to the complex nature of the ArrayList and Pex API usage, so here's an approach for the general use case: The assertions will be more specific and tailored based on the context and the requirement, instead of providing a broader, generic assertion.

For example, assuming you have the Add and Remove methods, you might provide output assertions like this:

```cs
Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount); 
// This assertion verifies that adding an item to an ArrayList does in fact increase its count

Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount - 1, New_arrListCount); 
// This assertion verifies that removing an item from an ArrayList does in fact decrease its count.
```

Also remember to replace 'ArrayList' with the appropriate namespace for the ArrayList in your project.
